From: MacVM Project <macvm@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] vmapple: match TCG counter to the Apple 24 MHz timebase

Boot114 exposes a 1 GHz generic counter with -cpu max, while every signed
DeviceTree CPU node and XNU's platform timebase use 24 MHz. This makes guest
deadlines and accounting run approximately 41.67 times faster than intended.
Set the TCG board counter frequency before CPU realization, including all
secondary CPUs. Leave hardware virtualization counters unchanged.

QEMU's existing integer-nanosecond timer scaling still rounds the 24 MHz
period to 41 ns (about 1.63% residual drift). Do not change only counter reads:
counter and timer expiry conversion must remain exact inverses. A future
rational-scaling change requires separate timer/overflow/WFxT tests.

diff --git a/hw/vmapple/vmapple.c b/hw/vmapple/vmapple.c
--- a/hw/vmapple/vmapple.c
+++ b/hw/vmapple/vmapple.c
@@ -638,6 +638,15 @@
         cpu = object_new(possible_cpus->cpus[n].type);
 
+        /*
+         * Apple firmware describes a 24 MHz platform timebase. The TCG
+         * "max" CPU otherwise defaults to 1 GHz, making XNU's timekeeping
+         * and deadlines diverge from the actual counter rate.
+         */
+        if (tcg_enabled()) {
+            object_property_set_int(cpu, "cntfrq", 24000000, &error_fatal);
+        }
+
         /* Must be registered before the CPU is realized. */
         vmapple_register_tcg_error_status_regs(ARM_CPU(cpu));
 
         object_property_set_int(cpu, "mp-affinity",
